1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,821
This is ECE 358, video 2 b. The topic is the network core, part 2. Video 2 a explained forwarding, store and forward, queueing, and circuit switching.

2
00:00:13,271 --> 00:00:19,266
After this video, you can count the users that a link supports with circuit and packet switching.

3
00:00:19,716 --> 00:00:21,657
You can compare the two methods.

4
00:00:22,107 --> 00:00:26,609
You can describe the structure of the Internet, as a network of networks.

5
00:00:27,059 --> 00:00:31,027
And you can solve problems 2 and 4 of problem set 1.

6
00:00:31,477 --> 00:00:37,834
We also solve question bank questions 22 to 24, and 26 to 31.

7
00:00:38,284 --> 00:00:41,825
Slide 28 compares the two methods with numbers.

8
00:00:42,275 --> 00:00:45,198
A link of 1 gigabit per second is shared.

9
00:00:45,648 --> 00:00:50,405
Each user sends at 100 Mbps when it is active.

10
00:00:50,855 --> 00:00:54,013
Each user is active 10 percent of the time.

11
00:00:54,463 --> 00:01:04,468
With circuit switching, each user reserves 100 Mbps all the time. User 1 gets the first 100 Mbps.

12
00:01:04,918 --> 00:01:14,689
1 gigabit per second is 1000 Mbps. 1000 divided by 100 is 10. So only 10 users fit.

13
00:01:15,139 --> 00:01:17,443
User 11 gets no circuit.

14
00:01:17,893 --> 00:01:24,229
With packet switching, nobody reserves anything. Suppose 35 users share the link.

15
00:01:24,679 --> 00:01:31,313
On average, 35 times 0.1, which is 3.5 users, are active.

16
00:01:31,763 --> 00:01:46,526
At this moment, 4 users are active. They need 4 times 100, which is 400 Mbps. That is not more than 1000 Mbps, so the link has enough capacity.

17
00:01:46,976 --> 00:01:59,285
The link is overloaded only when more than 10 users are active at the same time. 11 users need 1100 Mbps, which is more than 1000.

18
00:01:59,735 --> 00:02:05,069
The slide says that this probability is less than point 0 0 0 4.

19
00:02:05,519 --> 00:02:09,060
So packet switching supports many more than 10 users.

20
00:02:09,510 --> 00:02:19,067
This slide is optional. The slide calls the calculation a homework problem. But problem set 1 uses the same method, so learn it.

21
00:02:19,517 --> 00:02:24,851
Each user is active with probability p. The users are independent.

22
00:02:25,301 --> 00:02:30,463
P of n is the probability that exactly n of the M users are active.

23
00:02:30,913 --> 00:02:34,967
M choose n counts the ways to choose the n active users.

24
00:02:35,417 --> 00:02:40,302
p to the power n is the probability that those n users are active.

25
00:02:40,752 --> 00:02:48,560
1 minus p, to the power M minus n, is the probability that the other M minus n users are idle.

26
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:52,189
Here M is 35 and p is 0.1.

27
00:02:52,639 --> 00:03:02,687
The bars show P of n. Most of the probability is near 3 or 4 active users: about 0.22 and 0.20.

28
00:03:03,137 --> 00:03:12,737
Overload means more than 10 active users. So add P of n for n from 11 to 35. These bars are too small to see.

29
00:03:13,187 --> 00:03:16,707
The sum is 0.000424.

30
00:03:17,157 --> 00:03:28,527
That is about 0.04 percent of the time. The exact sum is a little more than point 0 0 0 4, so the slide gives a rounded value.

31
00:03:28,977 --> 00:03:43,377
Is packet switching always better? Data is often bursty: sometimes a user has data to send, and at other times it has none. Here are three bursty users. A blue slot means that the user sends.

32
00:03:43,827 --> 00:03:53,427
Packet switching is great for bursty data. All users share the buffer and the link capacity. The bottom row shows the load on the shared link.

33
00:03:53,877 --> 00:04:00,171
It is also simpler. There is no call setup, so no extra messages go before the data.

34
00:04:00,621 --> 00:04:06,253
This link carries 2 users. In 19 of the 20 slots, the load is 2 or less.

35
00:04:06,703 --> 00:04:12,783
But excessive congestion is possible. In slot 15, all 3 users are active.

36
00:04:13,233 --> 00:04:17,435
Then packets queue, and buffer overflow causes delay and loss.

37
00:04:17,885 --> 00:04:24,136
So packet switching needs protocols for reliable data transfer and for congestion control.

38
00:04:24,586 --> 00:04:34,997
The slide also asks how packet switching can give circuit-like behavior. Its answer is: it is complicated. The course studies some techniques later.

39
00:04:35,447 --> 00:04:44,449
Now the structure of the Internet. Hosts connect to the Internet through access Internet Service Providers, or access I S Ps.

40
00:04:44,899 --> 00:04:56,569
The access I S Ps must be interconnected, so that any two hosts, anywhere, can send packets to each other. Economics and national policies drive the result.

41
00:04:57,019 --> 00:05:05,360
The slides build the structure step by step. There are millions of access I S Ps. Here we draw N equal to 8.

42
00:05:05,810 --> 00:05:11,741
Option 1: connect each access I S P directly to each other access I S P.

43
00:05:12,191 --> 00:05:18,825
Each one needs a link to each of the other N minus 1. For one I S P, that is 7 links.

44
00:05:19,275 --> 00:05:32,438
N I S Ps times N minus 1 links counts each link twice, once from each end. So divide by 2. The number of links is N times N minus 1, divided by 2.

45
00:05:32,888 --> 00:05:41,997
For 8 access I S Ps, that is 8 times 7, divided by 2, which is 28 links. You can count them in the picture.

46
00:05:42,447 --> 00:05:55,674
For a million access I S Ps, it is 10 to the power 6, times 10 to the power 6 minus 1, divided by 2. That is about 5 times 10 to the power 11 links.

47
00:05:56,124 --> 00:06:00,839
The number grows as N squared. So the direct design does not scale.

48
00:06:01,289 --> 00:06:06,857
Option 2: connect each access I S P to one global transit I S P.

49
00:06:07,307 --> 00:06:12,405
Each access I S P needs only one link. So the number of links is N.

50
00:06:12,855 --> 00:06:17,271
For 8 access I S Ps, that is 8 links, not 28.

51
00:06:17,721 --> 00:06:22,927
The access I S P is the customer. The global I S P is the provider.

52
00:06:23,377 --> 00:06:26,961
The customer and the provider have an economic agreement.

53
00:06:27,411 --> 00:06:38,205
If one global I S P is a viable business, there will be competitors. Here are I S P A, B and C. Each one has its own access networks.

54
00:06:38,655 --> 00:06:46,762
But a host of I S P A cannot reach a host of I S P B yet. There is no link between the I S Ps.

55
00:06:47,212 --> 00:06:58,540
The competitors want to be connected. One way is an Internet exchange point, I X P. An I X P is a place where many I S Ps connect to each other.

56
00:06:58,990 --> 00:07:05,262
The second way is a peering link. A peering link is a direct link between two I S Ps.

57
00:07:05,712 --> 00:07:15,312
Next, regional networks may arise. A regional I S P connects the access networks of one region to a larger I S P.

58
00:07:15,762 --> 00:07:23,421
Last, content provider networks, for example Google, Microsoft and Akamai, may run their own network.

59
00:07:23,871 --> 00:07:33,492
The content provider network brings services and content close to the end users. It links directly to I X Ps and to access networks.

60
00:07:33,942 --> 00:07:39,787
Here is the result. At the center is a small number of well-connected, large networks.

61
00:07:40,237 --> 00:07:51,096
The first kind is the tier-1 commercial I S P. Examples are Level 3, Sprint, A T and T, N T T, Orange and Tata Communications.

62
00:07:51,546 --> 00:07:57,669
Tier-1 I S Ps have national and international coverage. They connect to each other.

63
00:07:58,119 --> 00:08:01,916
Below them are the I X Ps. Many networks meet there.

64
00:08:02,366 --> 00:08:04,755
Next are the regional I S Ps.

65
00:08:05,205 --> 00:08:09,621
At the bottom are the access I S Ps, which connect the hosts.

66
00:08:10,071 --> 00:08:16,919
The second kind of network at the center is the content provider network, for example Google or Facebook.

67
00:08:17,369 --> 00:08:26,628
It is a private network that connects its data centers to the Internet. It often bypasses the tier-1 and the regional I S Ps.

68
00:08:27,078 --> 00:08:32,155
Slide 39 shows a real tier-1 network: Tata Communications.

69
00:08:32,605 --> 00:08:40,733
Its backbone links connect many continents. This is the national and international coverage of a tier-1 I S P.

70
00:08:41,183 --> 00:08:46,517
The backbone has more than 1500 gigabits per second of capacity.

71
00:08:46,967 --> 00:09:02,028
1 gigabit is 10 to the power 9 bits. So 1500 gigabits per second is 1500 times 10 to the power 9, or 1.5 times 10 to the power 12 bits per second.

72
00:09:02,478 --> 00:09:06,403
It carries more than 750 petabits each month.

73
00:09:06,853 --> 00:09:11,248
Questions 22 to 24. Pause the video and try them.

74
00:09:11,698 --> 00:09:20,253
Question 22. Circuit switching reserves buffer, link bandwidth, and processing power on the network elements of the path.

75
00:09:20,703 --> 00:09:31,732
Question 23 compares the two methods. First, call setup. Circuit switching needs a call setup before the data. Packet switching needs no call setup.

76
00:09:32,182 --> 00:09:38,390
Resources. Circuit switching reserves dedicated resources. Packet switching shares them.

77
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:46,755
Performance. Circuit switching gives guaranteed performance. Packet switching can have delay and loss from congestion.

78
00:09:47,205 --> 00:10:01,925
Number of users. Circuit switching supports a fixed number of users: 10 on the 1 gigabit link of slide 28. Packet switching supports a variable number, many more than 10: for example 35.

79
00:10:02,375 --> 00:10:13,745
Question 24. The present-day Internet behaves like a packet switched network. Hosts break messages into packets, and routers forward them with no call setup.

80
00:10:14,195 --> 00:10:18,483
Questions 26 to 28. Pause the video and try them.

81
00:10:18,933 --> 00:10:29,003
Question 26. An I S P is a provider of a service: Internet access. Hosts connect to the Internet through access I S Ps.

82
00:10:29,453 --> 00:10:41,719
Question 27. A tier-1 I S P is a large commercial I S P at the center of the Internet. It is well connected and has national and international coverage.

83
00:10:42,169 --> 00:10:54,009
From the slide list: A T and T in North America, Orange in Europe, and N T T or Tata Communications in Asia. The slide does not give the home regions.

84
00:10:54,459 --> 00:11:04,614
Question 28. An I X P is an Internet exchange point: a place where many I S Ps connect to each other and exchange packets.

85
00:11:05,064 --> 00:11:17,459
The slides do not list the hardware of an I X P. This part is optional: in the textbook, an I X P has high speed switches that connect the routers of the I S Ps.

86
00:11:17,909 --> 00:11:22,303
Questions 29 to 31. Pause the video and try them.

87
00:11:22,753 --> 00:11:39,735
Question 29. Large content providers want to bring services and content close to the end users. At one I X P, they reach many I S Ps. So their traffic often bypasses the tier-1 and regional I S Ps.

88
00:11:40,185 --> 00:11:54,371
Question 30. A regional I S P connects the access networks of one region to larger I S Ps. It is the provider of the access networks and the customer of a larger I S P.

89
00:11:54,821 --> 00:12:05,787
Question 31 asks for two regional I S Ps in the Waterloo region. The slides do not name any, so this video does not answer that part.

90
00:12:06,237 --> 00:12:22,642
For the second part, use the slide definitions. A university network is an institutional access network, from slide 12. It connects its own hosts. So by the slides, it is an access network, not a regional I S P.

91
00:12:23,092 --> 00:12:39,476
Problem 2 of problem set 1. Users share a 2 Mbps link. Each user needs 300 kbps when it transmits, but it transmits only 12 percent of the time. Pause the video and try parts a to c.

92
00:12:39,926 --> 00:12:46,155
Part a. With circuit switching, each user reserves 300 kbps of the link.

93
00:12:46,605 --> 00:12:54,072
First write the link rate in kilobits per second. 2 Mbps is 2000 kbps.

94
00:12:54,522 --> 00:13:07,109
2000 divided by 300 is 6.67. The bar shows 6 full circuits of 300 kbps, and 200 kbps left over.

95
00:13:07,559 --> 00:13:19,121
Round down: 6 users. A seventh user would need 7 times 300, which is 2100 kbps. That is more than 2000.

96
00:13:19,571 --> 00:13:26,974
Part b. A user transmits 12 percent of the time. So the probability is p equals 0.12.

97
00:13:27,424 --> 00:13:33,781
Part c. The users are independent. So the number of users that transmit is binomial.

98
00:13:34,231 --> 00:13:44,322
The probability that exactly n of M users transmit is M choose n, times p to the power n, times q to the power M minus n.

99
00:13:44,772 --> 00:13:50,404
Here q is 1 minus p. 1 minus 0.12 is 0.88.

100
00:13:50,854 --> 00:13:53,990
Parts d to f. Pause the video and try them.

101
00:13:54,440 --> 00:13:58,963
Part d. M is not given, so the answer is a formula in M.

102
00:13:59,413 --> 00:14:11,658
Use the complement. First find the probability that 6 or fewer users transmit: the sum of the binomial terms for k from 0 to 6. Then subtract it from 1.

103
00:14:12,108 --> 00:14:17,249
The solution also gives the direct form: the sum for k from 7 to M.

104
00:14:17,699 --> 00:14:25,017
Part e. With M equal to 10, add four terms: P of 7, P of 8, P of 9, and P of 10.

105
00:14:25,467 --> 00:14:48,848
P of 7 is 10 choose 7, times 0.12 to the power 7, times 0.88 to the power 3. That is 120, times 3.583 times 10 to the power minus 7, times 0.6815. The result is 2.930 times 10 to the power minus 5.

106
00:14:49,298 --> 00:14:55,506
In the same way, P of 8 is 1.498 times 10 to the power minus 6.

107
00:14:55,956 --> 00:15:06,793
P of 9 and P of 10 are much smaller: 4.54 times 10 to the power minus 8, and 6.2 times 10 to the power minus 10.

108
00:15:07,243 --> 00:15:12,193
The sum is about 3.08 times 10 to the power minus 5.

109
00:15:12,643 --> 00:15:20,536
Part f. The solution gives no number. It only says that the probability of 7 or more users is very small.

110
00:15:20,986 --> 00:15:26,895
So more than 6 users can share the link with packet switching, without much extra delay.

111
00:15:27,345 --> 00:15:38,055
This chart is an added illustration, not part of the solution. It uses the formula of part d for M equal to 10, 20, 30 and 40.

112
00:15:38,505 --> 00:15:44,435
For 20 users, the overload probability is about 0.0067.

113
00:15:44,885 --> 00:15:49,323
For 40 users, it is about 0.2, which is too large.

114
00:15:49,773 --> 00:15:55,234
A reasonable M is the largest M whose overload probability you accept.

115
00:15:55,684 --> 00:16:10,404
Problem 4 of problem set 1. Users share a 2 Mbps link. Each user transmits at 1 Mbps, but only 20 percent of the time. Pause the video and try parts a and b.

116
00:16:10,854 --> 00:16:21,414
Part a. With circuit switching, each user reserves 1 Mbps. 2 divided by 1 is 2. So circuit switching supports 2 users.

117
00:16:21,864 --> 00:16:29,501
Part b. If two or fewer users transmit, they need at most 2 times 1, which is 2 Mbps.

118
00:16:29,951 --> 00:16:34,709
That is not more than the link rate. So there is essentially no queueing delay.

119
00:16:35,159 --> 00:16:42,391
If three users transmit, they need 3 Mbps. That is more than 2 Mbps.

120
00:16:42,841 --> 00:16:44,910
So packets queue before the link.

121
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:48,539
Parts c and d. Pause the video and try them.

122
00:16:48,989 --> 00:16:55,005
Part c. A user transmits 20 percent of the time. So the probability is 0.2.

123
00:16:55,455 --> 00:17:06,847
Part d. The three users are independent. So multiply: 0.2 times 0.2 times 0.2 is 0.008.

124
00:17:07,297 --> 00:17:15,019
The queue grows only when all three transmit, because 3 Mbps is more than 2 Mbps.

125
00:17:15,469 --> 00:17:24,387
So the queue grows during 0.008 of the time, which is 0.8 percent. This matches the solution.

126
00:17:24,837 --> 00:17:31,813
Here is the recap. One. For circuit switching, divide the link rate by the user rate and round down.

127
00:17:32,263 --> 00:17:44,231
Two. For packet switching, use the binomial probability. The overload probability is the probability of more than k active users. Often the complement is easier.

128
00:17:44,681 --> 00:17:58,398
Three. Packet switching shares resources and needs no call setup, but congestion causes delay and loss. Circuit switching needs a setup and reserves resources, with guaranteed performance.

129
00:17:58,848 --> 00:18:21,888
Four. A full mesh of access I S Ps needs N times N minus 1, over 2 links, so it does not scale. So the Internet uses transit I S Ps, I X Ps, peering links, regional I S Ps and content provider networks, with tier-1 I S Ps at the center. That is the end of video 2 b.
